#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""amarchy: stop babysitting your agents, manage them. Claude Code sessions in parallel, one git worktree each, on a server you reach from your phone and browser.

Every session is a branch + worktree off the latest main, with its Claude Code and a dev terminal in tmux on
this machine. The amarchy app (amarchy app on) shows them on your phone, as an app on the home screen, and in
a computer's browser, with the session's real terminals beside the list; it's reachable only on your Tailscale
network.

Every action in the app is one of these commands, so scripts and agents can do anything you can. Commands print
results to stdout and fail with a non-zero exit.

  amarchy help [command]                this, or the usage of one command (also amarchy <command> --help)

Projects (git repos directly inside AMARCHY_WORK):
  amarchy projects [--json]             your projects and their session counts
  amarchy projects --github [--json]    your GitHub repos (✓ = already added)
  amarchy projects order [project...]   the order your projects come in (the
                                        app's pickers): prints it, or puts these first, in this order; the
                                        rest keep theirs, and new ones go last, by name
  amarchy projects move <project> up|down|top|bottom
                                        move one project in that order
  amarchy projects move <project> before|after <other>
                                        the same, next to another one
  amarchy add <owner/repo|git url>      clone a project (prints its path)
  amarchy add --new <name> [--github]   start an empty project (and a private GitHub repo)

Sessions:
  amarchy new <project> [name]          create a session (name like wobbly-bakunin if omitted); prints its id
  amarchy new <project> [name] --prompt <text|->
                                        the same, with the first thing its Claude is asked (- reads it from stdin)
  amarchy new <project> [name] --model <model>
                                        on that model (opus, sonnet, haiku, a model id) instead of the new
                                        chat's default (the app's new chat picks it; Opus until you do)
  amarchy new <project> [name] --effort <effort>
                                        thinking that hard (low, medium, high, xhigh, max, or auto: Claude
                                        Code's default) instead of the new chat's (the app's new chat picks it)
  amarchy new <project> [name] --prompt <text|-> --attach <image>...
                                        with pictures (PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP files) going with that first
                                        message (the app's new chat: a screenshot pasted into it)
  amarchy new <project> [name] --agent <name> [--prompt <text|->]
                                        one of your agents from its start, called that (the app's New
                                        agent): it texts you, lives on, never a chat to complete
  amarchy new <project> [name] --on <machine> [...]
                                        the same on another machine of yours (amarchy machines): its project
                                        cloned there first from GitHub when it isn't there; prints <machine>:<id>
  amarchy send <id> <text|->            a message for a session's Claude, as if typed and sent (- reads it
                                        from stdin), once Claude is free: after the turn under way, not
                                        over a question it asks; a session asleep or whose Claude exited
                                        starts again with it. Waits until Claude has it (60 minutes at most)
  amarchy send <id> <text|-> [--no-wait] [--timeout <minutes>]
                                        the same, returning at once (it's delivered in the background), or
                                        waiting that long; from the session's own Claude it never waits
  amarchy send <machine>:<id> <text|->  the same to a chat on another machine of yours (amarchy machines):
                                        handed to it at once, delivered there once its Claude is free
  amarchy quiet [id] [--json]           the turns a timer, a script or another chat started that its Claude
                                        ended with NO_REPLY (nothing for you to see), hidden from the app:
                                        when, what woke it, the words it said why
  amarchy list [--compact]              sessions as JSON, in list order (task, age, and agent: the name its
                                        Claude goes by for other Claude sessions' SendMessage)
  amarchy list --all [--compact]        this machine's, then every paired machine's, each with its machine
                                        (one that doesn't answer: as last seen, machine_off)
  amarchy close [id] [--force]          close a session (current one if no id); --force discards uncommitted changes
  amarchy rename <title>                name the current session
  amarchy rename --id <id> <title>      the same for another session; --reset instead of a title goes back to the
                                        one made from Claude's title
  amarchy run                           run the project in the session's terminal (again: restart it)
  amarchy run --show | --set <command>  the project's run command (guessed from the repo until you set one)
  amarchy run <name>                    run another saved command of the project, the same way
  amarchy run --save <name> <command>   save one; --forget <name> drops it
  amarchy run --list                    the project's saved commands, name and command
  amarchy preview --run '<command>' <url> [--port <n>]... [--stop '<command>'] [--memory 2G] [--idle 3h]
                                        what this chat serves for you to look at (a dev server): amarchy runs
                                        the command as a service of the chat's own, outside its Claude (sleep
                                        doesn't stop it), capped in memory, and stops it after --idle unused or
                                        when the chat is completed (with the --stop command, for what it started
                                        elsewhere, like containers), checking its ports (the URL's, and each
                                        --port: an HMR port) are free. Waits until it answers; --no-start saves only.
                                        Its Preview button opens it, starting it again first when it isn't running.
                                        On a tailnet with HTTPS, give https://<this machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net:<port>
                                        (or http://localhost:<port>, which comes to that) and listen on 127.0.0.1:
                                        while it runs, amarchy serves the URL's port and each --port there over
                                        HTTPS (tailscale serve), at the same numbers; fast on a phone (HTTP/2)
  amarchy preview --name <name> ...     one of several (an app and its API, a storybook): each with a name of
                                        its own, and the button is Previews, a list to pick from. Without
                                        --name, it's the chat's only one (registering again changes it), and
                                        the commands below are about all of them
  amarchy preview --rename <name> [--name <its name>]
                                        name one (the one with no name, or the only one); nothing restarts
  amarchy preview start | restart | stop | status | log [--lines <n>] [--name <name>]
                                        start or stop it by hand, where it stands, what it wrote
  amarchy preview <url>                 an address only (a deploy, served elsewhere) on the Preview button.
                                        --clear stops it and takes it off; with nothing it prints the address.
                                        --id <id> for another session
  amarchy priority [high|normal|low|up|down] [--id <id>]
                                        how much the session matters (prints it without a level): the
                                        list keeps high ones pinned on top of Now, low ones in For later;
                                        new sessions are normal

Sleep (a session you're not using lets go of its Claude Code, and its memory, until you type in it or send
it something; its screen stays as it was, and what you type goes to Claude when it's back):
  amarchy sleep [id] [--force]          put a session to sleep (current one if no id); it says why not when
                                        something would be lost, and --force does it anyway
  amarchy sleep --all [--force]         every session that can sleep, except those open on a phone or in a
                                        browser
  amarchy sleep --after [minutes|off]   how long a session sits idle, open on no phone or browser, before it
                                        sleeps on its own (default 1; prints it without a value)
  amarchy wake [id]                     wake a session without typing in it

The app (your sessions on your phone, as an app on the home screen, and in a computer's browser, with real
terminals; reachable only on your Tailscale network):
  amarchy app on                        serve it on your tailnet (https://<this machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net,
                                        with tailscale serve), now and after every reboot; prints its address.
                                        Without Tailscale: this machine only, for the desktop app (its local
                                        key, ~/.local/state/amarchy/app/local.key; docs/guide.md, "Who gets in")
  amarchy app off                       stop serving it (the phones keep it, for when it's on again)
  amarchy app [--status]                whether it's on, where, and how many phones and browsers it notifies
  amarchy app --test                    send each phone a test notification
  amarchy notify <title> [body] [--session <id>] [--urgent]
                                        a notification of your own on each phone (and browser) the app
                                        notifies; a tap opens the session it's sent from (or
                                        --session's). --urgent: it stays until dismissed where the
                                        browser lets it (a computer's; iOS shows it as any other)

Machines (amarchy on several computers: your server, your desktop, a laptop; every machine's chats on one
board, in the app on any of them; docs/guide.md, "Machines"):
  amarchy machines [--json]             this machine, then the ones paired with it: each one's name, label,
                                        address and whether it answers (on, off, or update: another version)
  amarchy machines add <name|address>   pair with another machine of yours (its Tailscale name, e.g. omarchy, or
                                        its address): it must be yours, on your tailnet, running amarchy's app;
                                        both keep the other (~/.config/amarchy/machines.json, with their key)
  amarchy machines remove <name>        unpair (it's told, and forgets this one too)
  amarchy machines rename <name> <label>
                                        what the app calls a machine (this one, or another that answers):
                                        kept on that machine, so every machine shows the same
  amarchy machines icon <name> <icon>   a machine's icon on the board: server, desktop, laptop, cloud, tower or
                                        mini (kept on that machine; until picked, one that fits it)
  amarchy machines show icon|name       how the board shows a chat's machine: its icon (its name on a hover),
                                        or its name
  amarchy machines found [--json]       machines of yours on the tailnet that answer as amarchy, not paired yet
  amarchy move [<id>] --to <machine> [--when-free] [--secrets|--no-secrets]
                                        move a chat to another machine of yours, never on its own: once its
                                        Claude's step is over, what it hadn't committed goes as one WIP commit,
                                        its branch is pushed to origin and made its worktree there, and its
                                        conversation (resumed there), name, priority, seen and unread, run
                                        commands, previews and the files git ignores (not dependencies and
                                        builds) go with it; the secrets it asked for only with --secrets. It's
                                        taken off here once it's up there; anything wrong, it stays as it
                                        was. Busy: --when-free moves it once it's free (from the chat's own
                                        Claude, always so). Not your assistant or an agent. Prints <machine>:<id>
  amarchy move [<id>] --check --to <machine> [--json]
                                        what moving it would do, and whether it can now
  amarchy move [<id>] --cancel | --dismiss
                                        call off a move waiting for it to be free / forget why one didn't move

Theme (the app's look, on every phone and browser; Settings, Look in the app):
  amarchy theme [--show]                print it: the theme, the font and the wallpaper
  amarchy theme <name>                  wear one of amarchy's themes (Omarchy's palettes); Claude Code wears it too
                                        (a theme of yours, e.g. the look an Omarchy desktop sent, kept by
                                        amarchy migrate, keeps Claude Code's own colors)
  amarchy theme --font <name>           the font: JetBrains Mono (the default), Caskaydia Mono, Fira Code,
                                        System (the device's own), or one a theme of yours came with
  amarchy theme --wallpaper theme|<one of the theme's>|own|none|<picture>
                                        the theme's wallpaper: Omarchy's own (theme: the one picked for it,
                                        Omarchy's first until you pick; or one of its others, by name,
                                        see --list), your own (a picture you give it here or in the
                                        app, cut for a phone and a computer), or none
  amarchy theme --list [--json]         the themes, fonts and wallpapers, * on the ones worn, and how far
                                        Omarchy's wallpapers are fetched
  amarchy theme --refresh-wallpapers [--in-background]
                                        fetch Omarchy's wallpapers that aren't here yet from its repository on
                                        GitHub (install.sh and the app start it; until one is here, its theme
                                        wears a wallpaper drawn for it)
  amarchy theme --cut [--in-background] cut the fonts (text and icons apart, with fontTools) and your own
                                        wallpapers smaller for the app (install.sh and the app start it)
  amarchy theme --omarchy [--force] [--quiet] [--in-background]
                                        on an Omarchy desktop: make "Omarchy (live)" (omarchy-live) from its
                                        theme now (colors, menus, corners, wallpaper, font), keep it here and
                                        send it to your machines; Omarchy's hook and a path unit run it when
                                        Omarchy's look changes. Nothing changed and sent: nothing done
                                        (--force: again anyway)

Sound (the chime the app plays in a browser when a session needs you or is done):
  amarchy sound [--show]                print it
  amarchy sound <name> | off            set it (one of --list, or off for none)
  amarchy sound --list [--json]         every sound: amarchy's, then yours (added in the app, or put in
                                        ~/.config/amarchy/sounds)

Your assistant (one session that keeps your day rather than writing code: at the top of every list,
above the groups, bigger and with its picture, the other sessions of its project under it; it always
tells you when it needs you or is done, under its name and picture, with a chime of its own):
  amarchy assistant                     which session it is (id, then its name), or none
  amarchy assistant <id> [--name <name>]
                                        make a session your assistant (named after its project unless
                                        you name it); --name alone renames the one you have
  amarchy assistant off                 no assistant: it's a session like the others again
  amarchy assistant --picture <file>|off [--crop x,y,size]
                                        its picture in every list and by its notifications: yours (a PNG,
                                        JPEG or WebP, cut to a round square: the middle of it, or the square
                                        at x,y of that size in its pixels), or amarchy's own again
  amarchy sound --assistant [name|off]  its chime (herald until you pick one); prints it without one
  amarchy agents [--json]               your agents: your assistant, then the other chats that live on rather
                                        than finish (one that keeps your social media, say), each with its id,
                                        name, chime and picture
  amarchy agents <id> [--name <name>] [--picture <file>|off [--crop x,y,size]] [--sound <name>|off]
                                        make a chat one of your agents (named after its project unless you
                                        name it), or change one's name, picture or chime (your assistant's
                                        too). An agent has what your assistant has but your loops file: its
                                        place on top of every list, next to your assistant; its own picture
                                        (its initial until you give it one) and chime; it always tells you when
                                        it needs you or is done; boards and tasks on Tasks (amarchy board); a
                                        chat like a messaging app's (bubbles, typing…, unread counts), its
                                        Claude writing as someone you text; and it's never a mission to complete
  amarchy agents <id> off               not an agent any more: a chat like the others again
  amarchy missions [--goal <n>]         what you've merged (only merges count): today, your streak, best day,
                  [--reset]             all of them, your replies and badges; --goal sets how many a day you
                                        aim for; --reset starts from 0 (the old score kept in a backup)
                                        (0: none)
  amarchy alerts [all|needs-you|assistant]
                                        what the other sessions tell you about on your phones: when they
                                        need you or are done (all), only when one needs you (needs-you,
                                        the default: a finished one is marked done in the list, quietly),
                                        or nothing (assistant: your assistant and agents alone); prints it
                                        without one

Dictation (the app's mic button: what you say, written into the box you type in, by a speech-to-text model
on this machine that knows Spanish, English and 23 more languages and tells them apart by itself; on a
machine without it, by another machine of yours that has it, paired with amarchy machines):
  amarchy dictation [--status]          whether it's set up, where it's written down, and how to set it up if not
  amarchy dictation setup               fetch what dictation needs here: the speech engine (transcribe.cpp,
                                        about 20 MB, where no package brought it) and the model (Parakeet TDT
                                        0.6B v3, 740 MB), into the state; --in-background: and return at once;
                                        --engine: only the engine (what an install fetches; the model too
                                        where whisper.cpp's was set up before). Nothing to install or build,
                                        on x86_64 and aarch64 Linux
  amarchy dictation remove              delete the model and the engine fetched here again
  amarchy dictation log [--json]        the last dictations, from any phone or browser: how each went (which
                                        way it was written down, for which machine, how long it took) and
                                        what came of it
  amarchy dictation transcribe [--on <machine>] <file>
                                        write down a recording (anything ffmpeg reads), as a dictation would
                                        be: here, or on the machine that writes this one's down (or the one named)

Secrets (a key or password a project needs, given in the app, never in the chat; kept in
~/.config/amarchy/secrets, 0600, outside every repo; the session's commands, its terminal and its run
commands have each one as an environment variable, $NAME):
  amarchy secret ask <NAME> [--why <text>] [--all] [--again] [--timeout <seconds>]
                                        from a session's Claude: ask the user for it in the app (a card with
                                        a password field) and wait (590 s at most) until it's saved; prints
                                        only that it is, never the value. For this project's sessions, or
                                        --all projects' (the user can change that on the card); a name set
                                        already isn't asked again unless --again. Exits 1 if they decline,
                                        2 if they haven't answered yet. Never ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or Claude's
                                        other credentials: they'd switch Claude to API billing
  amarchy secret list [--json]          the secrets' names and for which project (never a value)
  amarchy secret set <NAME> [--project <name>|--all]
                                        keep one, its value read from stdin (or asked for, in a terminal);
                                        from a session, for its project unless --all
  amarchy secret forget <NAME> [--project <name>|--all]
                                        drop one (also: secret rm)

Automations (what runs for you by itself: every systemd user timer on this machine, your projects' jobs and
amarchy's own; a unit can explain itself with X-Amarchy-Name=, X-Amarchy-About=, X-Amarchy-When=,
X-Amarchy-Owner= and X-Amarchy-Children=<glob> lines, which systemd ignores; docs/guide.md, "Automations"):
  amarchy automations [--json]          each one: what it does, when it runs, its next run and how the last
                                        went, the one-off timers a job makes under it
  amarchy automations log <timer>       its last lines in the journal
  amarchy automations run <timer>       run its job now (its service), whatever its schedule
  amarchy automations pause <timer>     stop it until it's resumed, even across restarts (a drop-in of
                                        amarchy's own; no unit is changed or removed)
  amarchy automations resume <timer>    back on its schedule

Today (the app's home: what needs you, what's due, who you're waiting on, and every chat's goal and
state, with Close for the done ones; docs/guide.md, "Today"):
  amarchy today [--json]                the same in a terminal
  amarchy today --loops [<file>|off]    your loops file (your commitments and who you wait on, one per line:
                                        `- [ ] 2026-10-30 · what · nudge 10-29`, `- [ ] waiting · who · what ·
                                        since 10-23 · chase 10-27`, `- [ ] someday · what`, `- [x]` when done),
                                        or none; prints it without one. Today only reads it: its Done, Chase,
                                        Ask and New chat go to your assistant, which keeps it

Tasks: boards of what depends on you (a board's columns are its agent's: "For review", "Ready to post"...; its
cards are what your assistant and your agents need of you, each a block the app draws, buttons, a thread you
comment on; docs/guide.md, "Tasks: boards"). Your assistant and your agents keep them; another chat is refused:
  amarchy board [list] [--json]         the boards: id, lead, name, each column's count
  amarchy board set <board> [--title <text>] [--lead <agent>] [--check <command> | --no-check]
                                        [--column <name> [--yours] [--approval] [--done]
                                         [--button <label>[=<message>] [--to <column>] [--asks <words>] [--quiet]]...]...
                                        make a board (yours: its lead), or change it. Columns given are all of
                                        them, in order (none, a new board's: To do, Doing, Done). --yours: its
                                        cards wait on the user (Tasks counts them); --done: finished; --approval:
                                        a card gets there only by the user's hand, never an agent's, and is
                                        approved then (who, when, the sha256 of its content, signed); any change
                                        to it drops that, and it goes back. A column's buttons are on each of its
                                        cards: pressed, the message goes to the card's agent as the user's own
                                        ({title} {key} {board} {column} {words} in it; none: "<label>: “<title>”
                                        (board <board>, card <key>)"); --to moves the card; --asks asks the user
                                        for words first (a reason), added to the message; --quiet sends nothing.
                                        --check: a command run on the user's edit and approval first (the text on
                                        its input; $AMARCHY_CARD_ACTION edit or approve): failing, it refuses
                                        them, in its words
  amarchy board card <board> [--key <key>] [--title <text>] [--column <name>]
                                        [--xpost <text|-> | --block <kind> --body <text|->] [--attr <name>=<value>]...
                                        [--media <pictures>] [--handle <x handle>] [--name <name>] [--avatar <picture>]
                                        [--note <text|->] [--link [<label>=]<url>]... [--button <label>[=<message>]
                                        [--to <column>] [--asks <words>] [--quiet]]... [--until <when>] [--notify]
                                        [--base <sha256>] [--over-edit] [--clear-buttons] [--clear-links]
                                        put a card on a board (prints its id); --key (a card's key, or its id)
                                        changes that one in place: only what's given. Its block is any the app
                                        draws (xpost: as X shows it); a change to it is a revision on its thread,
                                        and drops its approval. A card's own buttons (up to 4) come after its
                                        column's; one without --to moves it to the board's done column. Refused
                                        over the user's edit unless --over-edit, over a change since --base
  amarchy board move <board> <card> <column>
                                        move a card (never into a column that needs the user's approval)
  amarchy board comment <board> <card> <text|->
                                        answer on a card's thread (what the user said there came to you as
                                        their message: "About “<card>” (board <board>, card <key>): ...")
  amarchy board show <board> [--card <card>] [--json]
                                        the board, or one card: its thread, approval {by, at, sha256}, sha256
  amarchy board approved <board> <card> [--text <file|->] [--json]
                                        exit 0 only when the user approved exactly what the card has now (and,
                                        with --text, that's its text): what a script checks before it acts
  amarchy board log [<board>] [--json] [--lines <n>]
                                        what was done on the boards, by whom: the user's edits (before and
                                        after), what they pressed and why, comments, moves
  amarchy board rm <board> <card>...    take cards down (nothing is sent)
  amarchy board drop <board>            the whole board
  amarchy task add --title <text> [--note <text>] [--link [<label>=]<url>]...
                                        [--button <label>[=<message>]]... [--until <when>] [--key <key>]
                                        [--notify] [--agent <id>]
                                        a task of the agent's on Tasks (prints its id): a card on its own board
                                        (its id's), with its picture and name, its title, a note, links (up to
                                        3: the page in a new tab; the task stays) and buttons (up to 4; none:
                                        Done). Pressed, a button sends its message to the agent as you'd send
                                        it (amarchy send: it wakes for it) and the task is done; with no message
                                        it sends "<label>: <title>". --until (2026-11-19T10:12-05:00, 10:12, 45m,
                                        2h, 1d): it goes by itself then. --key: the same agent's task with that
                                        key is changed in place, not added again. --notify: on your phones too
  amarchy task list [--agent <id>] [--json]
                                        the tasks open (id, agent, until, title), or one agent's
  amarchy task done <id>... | --agent <id>
                                        take tasks down (all of an agent's, with --agent): nothing is sent

Terminals:
  amarchy terminals [on|off]            Show terminals: whether the app shows the sessions' terminals (the
                                        Terminal tab, the panel beside the chat). Off on a new install,
                                        on for one from before; off, a session's menu still opens them
                                        (Advanced). Prints it without one

On Omarchy (a desktop of yours, on Omarchy 4; install.sh sets it up when Omarchy is there, its --uninstall
takes it away; docs/guide.md, "On Omarchy"):
  amarchy open [<id>|<machine>/<id>|amarchy://chat/<machine>/<id>]
                                        the desktop app (amarchy-desktop) at the front, on that chat: the
                                        window it has, else the app started. Super+D, Amarchy in the
                                        Omarchy menu and the Ⓐ in the bar run it. Without the desktop app,
                                        the board in a browser window (an Omarchy web app)
  amarchy key [<keys>|off|default] [--check]
                                        the key that brings amarchy to the front (amarchy open): Super+D,
                                        which Omarchy leaves free, or the one you pick (Super+Ctrl+M: one
                                        Omarchy uses gives way to amarchy while it's picked), or none; prints
                                        it without one, and what else this machine's Hyprland binds there
                                        (--check: only that). It changes at once, the Omarchy menu's hint too
  amarchy bar [--follow]                what the Ⓐ in Omarchy's bar shows, as JSON: how many chats need you
                                        and which (the first is what a click opens), from the board: this
                                        machine's own app server, else the first of your machines (amarchy
                                        machines), else AMARCHY_URL's. --follow prints it again on every
                                        change, from the board's events, and says when it doesn't answer
  amarchy tailscale [--json] [--no-open]
                                        Tailscale here, step by step: with none, amarchy's own node first
                                        (amarchy-tailnet: nothing to install; started for you), or the
                                        system's installed (Omarchy's installer on Omarchy); its service
                                        running, you allowed to drive it, signed in
                                        (the sign-in link, opened), on, and your tailnet's MagicDNS and HTTPS
                                        certificates (the admin page where they're turned on); what needs
                                        root it asks first, in a terminal, else says what to run. Exits 0
                                        once it's all ready. Settings → Tailscale in the app does the same
  amarchy tailscale switch [<account>]  the accounts signed in here (* the one in use), or switch to one
  amarchy ghostty [<id>] [term|claude]  a chat of this machine's in a real Ghostty window on its desktop: tmux
                                        attached to its terminal (or Claude's pane); again, that window to the
                                        front. In the app: a chat's menu → Advanced
  amarchy awake [on|off] [--json]       while a chat on this machine works, amarchy's app holds off its idle
                                        sleep (systemd-inhibit, "idle" only: the screen still locks, the lid
                                        still sleeps it) and lets go when none does: whether it holds it now,
                                        for which chats; off stops it, on (the default) starts it again

Updating:
  amarchy protocol-check [--broker]     run the chat engine's protocol test (about 3 minutes, a few cheap turns
                                        on your login) for the Claude Code installed, and record the result: a
                                        failure shows in Settings. --broker also runs a test session. amarchy
                                        update starts it when Claude Code has changed
  amarchy setup [--uninstall] [--force] amarchy's install.sh, as you: what's yours of amarchy (its timers, the
                                        skill for Claude Code, on Omarchy the Ⓐ, the key, the menu, the live
                                        theme). After installing the amarchy package (once), or to take it
                                        away again before removing the package (--uninstall)
  amarchy update                        to the latest amarchy on GitHub (origin's main): pull the checkout it
                                        runs from (when it's on main, with nothing uncommitted) and install it.
                                        It does it by itself every 5 minutes (AUTO_UPDATE=off in
                                        ~/.config/amarchy/host to stop that)

Plumbing:
  amarchy claude | asleep | woke <id>   the steps of the loop in a session's Claude pane (tmux): set up and
                                        start Claude; keep the session asleep on screen; the prompt you
                                        type in while it sleeps
  amarchy held <id>                     after its Claude exits, Enter starts it again
  amarchy attach <id> [claude|term]     show a session's Claude or terminal (tmux) in this terminal: what the
                                        app's terminals run, or you over ssh
  amarchy shell <id> [command]          the shell in a session's terminal (tmux starts it): with the project's
                                        secrets, read again at each prompt when they change (bash); with a
                                        command (a run command), that first
  amarchy sleep --idle                  put the sessions idle long enough to sleep (the timer runs it)
  amarchy reap <id>                     the rest of closing a session, detached from what closed it
  amarchy shorten <repo> <title>        cut Claude's title for the list down to "Keyword: a few words"
  amarchy hook <id> <state>             Claude Code hook: record working/waiting/idle and the conversation
                                        (start: idle, and tell Claude it's in amarchy)
  amarchy seen <id>                     you've opened a session that finished: its row drops the done mark
  amarchy paste [wl-paste options]      wl-paste for a session's Claude (bin/amarchy-paste): what you last
                                        pasted or copied in the app (phone or browser), so Ctrl+V pastes an
                                        image there
  amarchy dictation worker              keeps the dictation model loaded for the app's server (Transcriber)
  amarchy agent <id>                    the chat engine's broker in a session's Claude pane: Claude Code on
                                        claude -p, with a live state, a socket and a log of the conversation
                                        (docs/agent-protocol.md)
  amarchy agent engine <id> [chat|terminal] [--force]
                                        which engine a session's Claude runs on (prints it without one):
                                        its Claude starts again on the other one, the conversation kept,
                                        once it's idle (--force: even in a turn or a question)
  amarchy agent state|watch <id>        its live state (JSON) / everything Claude says, as it comes
  amarchy agent send <id> <text|-> [--next|--now|--later]
                                        a message: when Claude is free (or taken into the turn under way,
                                        ending it, or after it, queued by Claude)
  amarchy agent answer <id> <request> allow|always|deny [why]
                                        answer what Claude asks (--json <response> for a question's answers)
  amarchy agent interrupt <id> [--all]  Stop (--all: and drop what's queued)
  amarchy agent mode|model <id> <value> switch the permission mode or the model
  amarchy agent effort <id> <effort>    how hard it thinks: low, medium, high, xhigh, max, or auto
  amarchy agent control <id> <json>     any other control request, e.g. '{"subtype": "get_context_usage"}'
  amarchy app serve                     the app's server (amarchy-app.service runs it)
  amarchy migrate                       bring the state from before (a desktop's) up to date, once (the app's
                                        server and install.sh run it): the look the desktop sent becomes a
                                        theme of yours, "<name> (yours)", and is picked

Environment:
  AMARCHY_WORK         where your repos live (default ~/Work); every git repo directly inside is offered
  AMARCHY_WORKTREES    where worktrees go (default $AMARCHY_WORK/.worktrees/<repo>/<name>)
  AMARCHY_CLAUDE_ARGS  extra arguments for claude (else ~/.config/amarchy/claude-args, else "--permission-mode auto")
  AMARCHY_SHORT_NAMES  off to show Claude Code's titles as they are, not cut down to "Keyword: a few words"

~/.config/amarchy/host (KEY=value lines): NAME= and BIND= (how your devices reach what a session serves,
else this machine's Tailscale name and address), APP_USERS= (your other Tailscale logins the app lets
in, besides this machine's owner: yours on another tailnet), URL= (the address your other machines
reach this one's app at), APP_PORT= and APP_HTTPS= (the app's ports), APP_LINKS= (the port a Mac's
links out go through, 7398, or off), AUTO_UPDATE=off.

A session's Claude and terminal have AMARCHY=1, AMARCHY_SESSION (its id), $AMARCHY_PATH
(the worktree), $AMARCHY_NAME, $AMARCHY_BRANCH, $AMARCHY_REPO and $AMARCHY_ROOT (the
project's main checkout). From there, run, rename, close, priority and sleep act on that
session. Claude is told it's in amarchy when it starts (and that it can message the other
sessions' Claude), and the amarchy skill (skill/, linked into ~/.claude/skills by install.sh)
has the rest.

Run commands are saved per project, start in the session's worktree and can use the same
variables. Quote them when setting from a shell:
  amarchy run --set '$AMARCHY_PATH/install.sh && systemctl --user restart amarchy-app'
(install.sh asks before replacing an install that runs from another session's worktree,
and stops without a terminal to ask in; --force goes on anyway. Closing the session an
install runs from puts it back on the main checkout.)

State: ~/.local/state/amarchy/.
"""

import base64
